think Standfast first: read crux/REDS.md. Every living row (80, 83, 84, 85) is closed or released, prose notes #81/#82/#86/#87/#78/#80/#73/#74 all CLOSED or released, and #75 rests as custody-gated errata (the deep-debride history rewrite, Keaton's hand). No agent-closable red open, so the line is clear to grow new work -- new constructive work may begin.
think Running thread: the top three session-logs/README rows and REMEMBER's live edge agree -- Season G open-media is the live family, the last lap landed image/regions.rye GREEN. The regions log named the next siblings crux-first: a DISK-element builder, a WATERSHED or a local-maxima medial-axis reader off the distance field, a size-HISTOGRAM/region-picker.
think Crux-first among equals-Lindy: the LOCAL-MAXIMA MEDIAL-AXIS reader off the freshly-built distance field. It is the natural consumer of distance.rye (whose own doc-comment says its ridge IS the medial axis), a genuine algorithm distinct from zhang_suen thinning (metric ridge vs. topological peeling), and the hardest-solvable that opens the watershed. The disk-element builder is the easiest not the crux; the watershed is larger than one SLC and wants markers this rung can seed.
think Design: compose not braid. medial_mask(dm) reads a distance.DistanceMap and marks a foreground pixel iff no eight-neighbor holds a strictly greater distance (a local max, plateaus kept). colorize(dm) renders the medial axis TRANSFORM -- the spine alone ramped by depth, a non-ridge foreground pixel black -- distinct from distance.colorize which ramps the whole field. medial_axis_of(src) is the convenience over a mask. This rung owns only the ridge test and the two renders; distance owns the field, branch.is_on the binary door, qoi the buffer, color.mix the ramp.
think The honest subtlety, named in code/README/card not hidden: the distance ridge is NOT guaranteed unit-width. An even-thickness bar reads one depth across its whole cross-section, so its entire width is a local maximum and the ridge comes back two pixels wide -- where zhang_suen thins the same bar to a single row. The two routes are complements: thinning keeps topology and loses thickness; the ridge keeps thickness and loses the unit-width guarantee. Named the TWO-WIDE crux as the decisive proof of this.
obs GREEN on the FIRST metal build and run, no algorithm reds. Cruxes proven: the BLOCK (a 3x3 block reads depth 6 center / 3 ring, medial axis the single center -- the same point zhang_suen thins the block to, reached by the metric ridge); the LINE (uniform depth 3, ridge keeps it whole, anti-erosion); the TWO-WIDE strip (two pixels thick, the non-unit-width proof); the LONE-DOT (its own spine); the degenerate all-background field (empty axis); the MAT-RENDER (center at max depth full white, a non-ridge ring pixel black, round-tripping the open QOI codec); the convenience equalling field-then-ridge; an empty-field refusal by name; determinism.
obs TAME bans clean (no compound assert this time -- kept each assert atomic from the start); width-check exit 0; plain 7-bit ASCII in both new files; living_docs_lint advisory-GREEN; living_card_ascii_witness GREEN (REMEMBER + REDS pure ASCII, mojibake control caught). Two asserts-or-more per function throughout, // invariant on each.
